# Project: Tessera — a game backend + mobile Sequence game

  You are helping me build a small but real multiplayer game backend, and a mobile
  client for the board game **Sequence** to exercise it. I am doing this to learn
  backend/game-server systems concepts (authoritative state, rooms, WebSockets,
  write-ahead logging, persistence tiers, matchmaking, reconnection). Treat this as
  a learning project: explain the *why* behind design choices, not just the code.

  ## Tech stack (already decided — do not re-litigate)
  - Backend: **Go** (standard library first; add deps only when justified).
  - Mobile client: **Flutter (Dart)**, iOS + Android.
  - Persistence: start minimal — in-memory state + a write-ahead log file + SQLite.
    Redis/Postgres/horizontal scaling come later as optional blocks.

  ## How we work together (STRICT — this matters most)
  1. Build **one building block at a time**, in the order below.
  2. For each block: (a) briefly restate what it does, (b) implement it, (c) write
     tests, (d) show me how to run the tests and what "green" looks like.
     **Do not start the next block until I confirm the current one passes.**
  3. Enforce strict inward-pointing layers:
     engine (pure logic) ← room manager ← transport ← persistence ← infra.
     The engine must NOT import networking or DB packages.
  4. Use **seeded RNG** everywhere randomness appears (shuffles), so tests are
     deterministic and reproducible.
  5. Run concurrency-sensitive code under `go test -race`.
  6. Prefer clarity over cleverness; comment the non-obvious systems decisions.
  7. If a decision is genuinely mine to make, ask a focused question instead of
     guessing. Otherwise pick a sensible default and note it.

  ## The game (get the model right)
  Sequence: a 10×10 board (100 cells). Every non-jack card appears on the board
  twice; the 4 corners are wild ("free") spaces belonging to everyone. On your turn:
  play a card from hand → place a chip on a matching OPEN cell → draw a replacement.
  - **Two-eyed jacks** (J♦, J♣) = wild: place a chip on any open cell.
  - **One-eyed jacks** (J♥, J♠) = remove one opponent chip (not one already part of
    a completed sequence).
  - **Dead card**: if both board cells for a card you hold are occupied, you may
    discard it and draw, once per turn.
  - **Win**: form a run of **5 chips in a row** horizontally, vertically, or
    diagonally; corners count as wild for any player.
  - v1 scope: **2 players**, `sequences_to_win` configurable (default 2; allow 1 for
    fast test games).

  ## Building blocks — backend
  - **B0 Skeleton & tooling**: Go module, repo layout, Makefile, `/healthz` HTTP
    endpoint, structured logging, config with seeded RNG.
    Gate: `curl /healthz` = 200; `go test ./...` runs clean.
  - **B1 Pure game engine (no I/O)**: Card, Deck, Board (cell↔card map + corners),
    Player, Chip, GameState; rules for deal, play-card→place-chip, both jack types,
    dead-card swap, draw, sequence detection, win. All pure functions.
    Gate: extensive table-driven tests — sequence detection in every direction incl.
    corners, both jack behaviors, illegal/out-of-turn moves, win conditions.
    (This is the crown jewel — over-test it before anything else touches it.)
  - **B2 In-memory room manager**: each room = one match's GameState owned by a
    single goroutine fed by a command channel (actor model, no shared-memory locks
    on the hot path). Commands: Join, PlayMove, Leave. Idempotency: each move carries
    move_id + expected turn/seq; reject duplicates and out-of-turn moves.
    Gate: drive a full 2-player game in-process; pass `go test -race` under load.
  - **B3 Transport (HTTP + WebSocket)**: REST (create/list matches, GET state for
    reconnect) + WebSocket (per-connection goroutine; client sends move requests,
    server is authoritative and broadcasts results). Typed JSON envelope
    {type, seq, payload}.
    Gate: integration test — two WS clients play a full game through the server;
    drop a socket mid-game, reconnect via GET state, resume.
  - **B4 Durability — WAL + replay**: append-only per-match event log; every accepted
    command is appended (configurable fsync policy) BEFORE apply/ack. On startup,
    replay the WAL to rebuild state; apply idempotently via move_id/seq.
    Gate: kill the process mid-game, restart, assert full recovery; assert duplicate
    replay is safe.
  - **B5 Cold tier — SQLite + write-behind**: persist finished matches, history, and
    player stats to SQLite asynchronously in batches; checkpoint/truncate the WAL
    after a successful flush.
    Gate: history+stats land in SQLite; WAL checkpoints; a crash between WAL and
    SQLite still recovers.
  - **B6 Matchmaking, presence, light auth**: anonymous player id + lightweight
    token; in-memory matchmaking queue (join → paired into a room); basic presence.
    Gate: two clients matchmake, get paired, play; identity survives reconnect.

  ## Building blocks — mobile (Flutter)
  - **M1** app skeleton → hit `/healthz`, show server status.
  - **M2** identity + lobby (create/join/matchmake via REST).
  - **M3** board rendering — 10×10 grid, card faces, chips (render a static GET
    state first).
  - **M4** WebSocket wiring — live state in, tap-card→tap-cell moves out.
  - **M5** full game UI — hand, turn indicator, both jack flows, dead-card, win
    screen.
  - **M6** reconnection UX.
    Gate per step: manual play on emulator against the tested backend; finish by
    running two emulators playing each other.

  ## Later / optional (do not start unless I ask)
  Redis for shared matchmaking/presence/pub-sub + leaderboard; multi-process
  horizontal scaling with sharded rooms; JSON→protobuf; Prometheus metrics; Postgres.

  ## Proposed repo layout
  tessera/
    backend/  cmd/tessera/ , internal/{engine,room,transport,wal,store,match}
    mobile/   (Flutter app)
    docs/     (message protocol + design notes)

  ## Start here
  First show me a short plan for **B0 + B1** and wait for my "go". Then implement B0,
  show the test gate, and stop for my confirmation before B1. Do not scaffold future
  blocks ahead of time.
